.TH SYSSCOPED 8 "2026-11-15" "sysscope 0.0.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sysscoped \- SysScope process-monitor daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysscoped
[\fB\-\-enable\-act\fR] [\fB\-\-syscalls\fR count|off] [\fB\-\-record\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysscoped
loads eBPF probes that observe processes (programs started, files opened,
renamed and deleted, connections, syscall counts), keeps a bounded in-memory
process table, runs the detection rules, and serves the
.BR sysscope (1)
and
.BR sysscope-gui (1)
clients on \fI/run/sysscope/sysscope.sock\fR (mode 0660, group
\fBsysscope\fR). It makes no network connections.
.PP
It is normally run by systemd as \fBsysscoped.service\fR, which is installed
but not enabled:
.B systemctl enable \-\-now sysscoped
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-act
Allow the experimental act mode (pause, resume, kill on request).
.TP
.BR \-\-syscalls " count|off"
Per-process syscall counts (default count).
.TP
.BI \-\-record " FILE"
Write every event to FILE (development; see docs/PRIVACY.md).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sysscope/rules.toml, /etc/sysscope/allow.toml, /etc/sysscope/sysscoped.toml
Local overrides of the built-in rules, allowlist and settings.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sysscope
Glasshouse runs and reports; R100 baselines if enabled.
.SH EXIT STATUS
3 when this kernel cannot run SysScope (for example no BTF), with an explanation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sysscope (1),
.BR sysscope-glasshouse (8),
/usr/share/doc/sysscope/THREAT_MODEL.md
